WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.110 --> 00:00:03.300
Film Courage: How much did it cost you to
start Film Threat?

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:06.630
Chris Gore, Founder/Owner of FilmThreat/Author/Filmmaker:
Oh wow… well it cost me nothing because

00:00:06.630 --> 00:00:12.129
I wrote a bad check and it bounced oh okay
this is when we would write checks for things

00:00:12.129 --> 00:00:17.780
so I yeah to start Film Threat I mean I guess
the real investment was I bought a book about

00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:24.590
how to publish a magazine because based on
my college education I kind of felt like well

00:00:24.590 --> 00:00:30.900
I'm paying them all this money to buy really
expensive versions of books and then regurgitate

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:36.690
the contents of those books in the form of
essays or questionnaires when I can just buy

00:00:36.690 --> 00:00:42.440
the books and read those books recreationally
to because being filled with knowledge was

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:47.680
the thing that was important to me not proving
to a professor that I had read the book but

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:53.970
I bought a book from folio magazine which
is the magazine about the magazine business

00:00:53.970 --> 00:00:59.329
it's like variety for magazines and I bought
a book from them about how to publish a magazine

00:00:59.329 --> 00:01:06.000
and read that book and realize there was no
way I was going to be able to do it but I

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:13.660
the first issue was six pages and I had put
it together I had like the logo which I had

00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:20.790
created and sort of a fan zine I like the
title 'Film Threat,' it sounded punk rock

00:01:20.790 --> 00:01:21.790
and

00:01:21.790 --> 00:01:27.200
I was a big fan of I was a big fan of like
old school fanzines they were mostly about

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:32.540
music and skateboarding and while I loved
music could not skateboard I loved movies

00:01:32.540 --> 00:01:38.500
I wanted to do a fanzine about movies Film
Threat just seemed to fit with all of those

00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:43.060
so I met a guy named Andre Seawood at my school
at Wayne state university and he had written

00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:49.049
some very thoughtful essays very complex about
politics and film most of which I didn't understand

00:01:49.049 --> 00:01:54.020
but I thought well look here's a platform
I’ll do jokes and come up with sort of comedic

00:01:54.020 --> 00:01:59.100
things and movie parody posters and whatnot
I’ll write some reviews myself and you can

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:03.310
put your essays in this and we put out this
magazine we put out the first issue which

00:02:03.310 --> 00:02:08.340
was six pages and I wrote a check it was I
believe the check was for like maybe 50 and

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:15.239
it it bounced there were only 500 copies of
the first issue of Film Threat that were published

00:02:15.239 --> 00:02:20.030
but but sorry who did you sorry interrupt
but who did you write the check to Kinkos

00:02:20.030 --> 00:02:25.010
so yeah I wrote I wrote the check to Kinko's
and it bounced and I there were charges from

00:02:25.010 --> 00:02:35.040
the bank anyways eventually I then paid that
off and then I did come up with a way by selling

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:40.540
advertising to be able to pay for the magazine
it wasn't expensive at that level Andre and

00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:49.230
I parted ways later and I wanted to really
get the magazine up to a much higher not like

00:02:49.230 --> 00:02:55.959
a 12-page thing I wanted to do something more
substantial so I went into a printer and I

00:02:55.959 --> 00:03:02.790
said I learned about this thing called credit
and I went into a printer in in Detroit and

00:03:02.790 --> 00:03:08.280
I said could you print this movie on cred
print this magazine on credit and I had walked

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:13.770
in with a business suit that I bought from
like value village was the name of the place

00:03:13.770 --> 00:03:18.080
which was sort of a salvation army I bought
it at Value Village for five dollars I walked

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:22.140
in with this suit I looked really professional
even though I was very boyish looking face

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:28.830
at the time and I just said well you printed
on credit and they said yes and I did this

00:03:28.830 --> 00:03:34.040
I did the same scam to another printer and
then another printer another printer to the

00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:39.000
point where then like the sheriff's department
was like look you you have to pay these printers

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:45.410
money I had them coming after me at the time
i'd amassed a huge record collection and sold

00:03:45.410 --> 00:03:50.140
it all to be able to pay the printers off
so I basically just sold like all the stuff

00:03:50.140 --> 00:03:55.159
that I had just to be able to keep the creditors
off my back and then that actually got the

00:03:55.159 --> 00:04:01.450
attention of an investor who thought that
Film Threat like oh this is crazy story of

00:04:01.450 --> 00:04:08.420
this kid who was like defrauding like printers
in Detroit to print a magazine and so he decided

00:04:08.420 --> 00:04:14.510
to invest in it and then so there are very
stages of Film Threat there's the zine stage

00:04:14.510 --> 00:04:22.400
getting it printed at like off of a copy machine
then the more formal magazine stage and then

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:30.430
the stage where I sold the magazine in and
eventually sold it to Larry Flynt and there's

00:04:30.430 --> 00:04:36.839
a whole story there where I licensed it to
him for a dollar which I never got but he

00:04:36.839 --> 00:04:42.610
didn't pay me anything for the magazine what
I ended up getting was I got free advertising

00:04:42.610 --> 00:04:48.379
in the magazine and I got a a budget to work
with actually so I sort of ca I sort of split

00:04:48.379 --> 00:04:54.139
Film Threat into two magazines one being Film
Threat magazine which was published by LFP

00:04:54.139 --> 00:04:58.699
Larry Flynt publications and the second magazine
was called Film Threat video guide which we

00:04:58.699 --> 00:05:03.649
and we had a rule that any movie that was
made for under say a hundred thousand dollars

00:05:03.649 --> 00:05:07.869
really low budget or underground films we
put those in Film Threat video guide and then

00:05:07.869 --> 00:05:12.219
Film Threat magazine did the bigger budget
indie movies right the ones were made for

00:05:12.219 --> 00:05:18.470
about a million or so this was in the 90s
and so we had sort of cleared delineations

00:05:18.470 --> 00:05:22.759
and then we would do a section in Film Threat
called underground where we would cover the

00:05:22.759 --> 00:05:29.180
magazines or highlights of the the movies
that were covered in Film Threat video guide

00:05:29.180 --> 00:05:33.919
so they were like these sister publications
and that led to creating a distribution label

00:05:33.919 --> 00:05:39.409
where we put out people like Todd Phillips
his first documentary called hated about Gigi

00:05:39.409 --> 00:05:44.139
Allen and the Murder Junkies so we put out
Todd Phillips first documentary film on VHS

00:05:44.139 --> 00:05:51.869
in the 90s and that sort of kicked off our
company we put out about 30 different films

00:05:51.869 --> 00:05:57.770
on VHS and Film Threat video guide basically
championed those movies and the reason that

00:05:57.770 --> 00:06:02.919
company started is because filmmakers would
come to us and they would say we'd love to

00:06:02.919 --> 00:06:09.699
advertise our movie that we're releasing on
VHS ourselves in Film Threat video guide we'd

00:06:09.699 --> 00:06:14.839
say okay well advertising costs this much
a page and they'd say we don't have the money

00:06:14.839 --> 00:06:18.379
so we figured out a way like well how about
this we're not going to get the rights to

00:06:18.379 --> 00:06:23.479
your movies we're just going to give you free
advertising we'll do fulfillment for you and

00:06:23.479 --> 00:06:29.509
then we'll pay you a larger percentage of
what the money we take in right so we did

00:06:29.509 --> 00:06:36.449
VHS duplication and we were basically giving
we never owned the movies per se it was like

00:06:36.449 --> 00:06:42.930
a licensed deal and then we would sell these
filmmakers movies on VHS at the time and built

00:06:42.930 --> 00:06:51.039
up like York Bucharet's film necromantic we
put out his second film Dirtotis King and

00:06:51.039 --> 00:06:55.419
he was a german filmmaker that helped him
get distribution in other countries so we

00:06:55.419 --> 00:07:01.650
championed smaller films made by like Richard
Kern is one of the filmmakers among others

00:07:01.650 --> 00:07:08.889
Todd Phillips put out his documentary and
then we put out a series of short films put

00:07:08.889 --> 00:07:13.860
out by the New York underground film festival
so we had these very distinct delineations

00:07:13.860 --> 00:07:20.110
between the two Film Threat was the more mainstream
snarky giving the middle finger to Hollywood

00:07:20.110 --> 00:07:26.649
bigger budget indie movies and then Film Threat
video guide was the smaller underground films

00:07:26.649 --> 00:07:31.139
and movies that we actually were distributing
and helping the filmmakers make money selling

00:07:31.139 --> 00:07:36.249
their movies how did you meet Larry Flynt
how I met Larry Flynt was after he noticed

00:07:36.249 --> 00:07:46.379
me I was writing and comedy for Hustler Magazine
they had this section in the front that was

00:07:46.379 --> 00:07:51.039
just sort of it was like mad magazine but
a very, very adult version of it and I wrote

00:07:51.039 --> 00:08:00.819
jokes for it and he loved what I did he thought
that I did like movie parodies I did a parody

00:08:00.819 --> 00:08:07.659
a film parody The Revenge of Anne Frank which
was Anne Frank as a zombie risen from the

00:08:07.659 --> 00:08:17.229
dead to kill nazi skinheads I grew up in Detroit
around and skinheads existed that would and

00:08:17.229 --> 00:08:22.979
I thought that they were very obnoxious I
did not like them so that inspired me to come

00:08:22.979 --> 00:08:30.059
up with that concept and that was one among
many of the the jokes that I put in that section

00:08:30.059 --> 00:08:38.019
anyways he really liked the stuff that I was
writing in there and I pitched to Jim Coles

00:08:38.019 --> 00:08:45.180
who was the CFO of the company I said look
I said Hustler magazine is like a punk rock

00:08:45.180 --> 00:08:49.930
adult magazine right you have Playboy and
then you got Hustler punk rock at the time

00:08:49.930 --> 00:08:54.540
they published a magazine called RIP which
was a heavy metal magazine and RIP is like

00:08:54.540 --> 00:09:01.630
this angry punk rock metal magazine it's sort
of the opposite of rolling stone I said what

00:09:01.630 --> 00:09:09.579
you need you need a punk rock movie magazine
you've got adult content you've got rip magazine

00:09:09.579 --> 00:09:15.940
music how about film and and that concept
was taken to Jim Coles who ran the company

00:09:15.940 --> 00:09:27.680
at the time and he loved the concept so I
met Larry Flynt it was it was I mean it was

00:09:27.680 --> 00:09:32.300
strange to meet him I mean here here I am
a college dropout he gave me an opportunity

00:09:32.300 --> 00:09:39.759
and for for that I will be forever grateful
and his his fight for the first amendment

00:09:39.759 --> 00:09:47.670
is admirable and it's a lot of the freedoms
we enjoy the freedom of the press things that

00:09:47.670 --> 00:09:52.759
we're able to say are specifically because
of Larry I mean he he really nearly gave up

00:09:52.759 --> 00:10:01.930
his life for that if about his history so
he was shot yeah I mean he was shot and crippled

00:10:01.930 --> 00:10:08.839
so but Larry was fearless I mean that really
inspired me with Film Threat at the time as

00:10:08.839 --> 00:10:14.839
well I mean my passion wasn't necessarily
adult content but my passion was film and

00:10:14.839 --> 00:10:21.060
just that he gave me a voice to be able at
a budget to be able to put out this magazine

00:10:21.060 --> 00:10:29.560
at the time was pretty incredible so I’m
really grateful will always be forever grateful

00:10:29.560 --> 00:10:35.470
for him giving me a boost and and getting
Film Threat magazine on newsstands all over

00:10:35.470 --> 00:10:40.379
the country didn't anyone talk you out of
starting Film ThreatI know you you started

00:10:40.379 --> 00:10:45.850
it with someone yes college okay my family
tried to talk me out of doing Film Threatthey

